{
  "name": "encoding-reflection-unwritten-encoded-field",
  "description": "A field an encoder emits that nothing writes is reported at the reachability class possible rather than certain: deleting it removes a key from the output, which the analysis cannot prove nothing reads. That holds for an encoding entry point of a standard encoder, reached directly or through a function of the program that hands its parameter on, and for any destination outside the analyzed program, whatever its name: a function or method outside it given the value at an empty-interface parameter, as a request context's JSON method is, and code outside it that receives the value as an interface-typed result of a method the program implements for an interface the outside package declares. A field whose tag makes every encoder that reads it leave its zero value out never reaches the output, so it keeps the class certain, each encoder judged by its own rule. encoding/json and encoding/xml leave out under `omitempty` a false, a zero number, an empty string and the like by kind, whatever methods the type has, but not a struct. encoding/json/v2 leaves out under `omitempty` only a value that encodes as null, an empty string, object or array, so it writes a false and a value a JSON method of its type encodes, and a field both JSON packages encode is left out only where both leave it out. Under `omitzero` both JSON packages call the type's IsZero method where it has one, whose answer the analysis does not know without running it, so such a field keeps the class possible whatever its IsZero returns, and the encoder keeps the IsZero method it calls. encoding/json and encoding/xml call no IsZero under `omitempty`, so an IsZero method only such a field's type declares is reported, even where the field's JSON name contains the word omitzero. Such a destination outside the analyzed program may hand the value to a JSON encoder, which calls IsZero under `omitzero`, or to a YAML encoder, which calls it under the `yaml` tag's `omitempty`, so it keeps that method: a wrapper named Render and a context's JSON method that call json.Marshal keep the IsZero of an `omitzero` field they are given. A type that encodes itself through a method an embedded field promotes, as a struct embedding time.Time does, has that embedded field read, because calling the method reads it. A type whose own JSON method the encoder calls ahead of a text method an embedded field promotes leaves that field unread. A formatting function is no encoder: a field it prints is read at the call, since fmt prints every field of a struct whether or not anything writes it. Under a production sweep a test's encoder lowers only a field a test file declares: a field of the command that only a test encodes or fills is a test-only use at the class certain.",
